[size=5][b]Reliquary[/b][/size]

Steam gives you one build of a game: the current one. Reliquary lets you pick a different one.

You sign in with your own Steam account, choose a game and a version, and it downloads that build from Steam's own content servers into a folder you choose. It can also change a game you already have installed from one build to another, in place.

[img]IMAGE_URL_1[/img]

[size=4][b]Why[/b][/size]

Mods are built against a particular build of a game. When the game updates, the mod breaks, and the usual advice is to find a downgrade patcher, a depot downloader with a manifest ID pasted out of a forum post, or a copy of the old files from somewhere you would rather not download files from.

The builds are still on Steam's servers. If you own the game, you can already download them. Reliquary is a front end for doing that without needing to know a manifest ID.

[size=4][b]What it does[/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Downloads a chosen version.[/b] Pick from the versions in the catalog and install it into any folder. Your existing install is left alone.
[*][b]Changes an existing install.[/b] Point it at a game Steam already has on disk and switch that install to another build, without downloading the whole game again.
[*][b]Transfers only what differs.[/b] Changing Skyrim Special Edition from the current build to 1.6.1130 leaves 13.81 GB untouched, moves 0.97 GB that is already on disk into new positions, and downloads 0.21 GB. That is measured on a real install, not an estimate.
[*][b]Knows which build you have[/b] by hashing the game executable and looking the hash up, rather than trusting Steam's own record of what it installed. That record goes stale as soon as files are swapped by hand.
[/list]

[img]IMAGE_URL_2[/img]

[size=4][b]Requirements[/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Windows 64-bit.
[*]A Steam account that owns the game. Reliquary asks Steam for the files under your licence; it cannot get you anything you have not bought.
[*]Disk space for the version you are installing.
[/list]

Nothing else. The download is a folder with an .exe and a bundled Java runtime in it, so there is no installer to run and no separate runtime to fetch. It keeps a sign-in token and a couple of preferences in [b].config\reliquary[/b] and [b].local\share\reliquary[/b] under your user folder. Games go wherever you tell it.

[size=4][b]Signing in[/b][/size]

Two ways: scan a QR code with the Steam mobile app, or type your username and password and approve the Steam Guard prompt. The session token is stored locally so you do not sign in every launch. Your password is not stored, and it is sent to Steam encrypted with Steam's own RSA key, the same way the official client does it.

[img]IMAGE_URL_3[/img]

[size=4][b]Changing an install[/b][/size]

Select an installed game, pick the version you want, and press the button. Reliquary hashes what is on disk, works out which pieces the target build needs that are already there, stages the ones that have to move, and writes the rest.

Two things to know before you use it:

[list]
[*][b]It rewrites the install in place.[/b] There is no backup and no rollback. That is deliberate: the way back is to switch again, and a 15 GB copy of a game you can rebuild in a few minutes is not worth the disk.
[*][b]Steam will update the game again[/b] the next time it feels like it. Set the game to "Only update this game when I launch it" in Steam if you want the build to stay put.
[/list]

If the build you have is not one the catalog recognises, which is what you get after a manual downgrade or after Steam ships a build newer than the catalog, the button offers a forced switch instead. It checks every file against the target and fetches what does not already match. That downloads more than an ordinary switch and much less than a fresh install.

[img]IMAGE_URL_4[/img]

[size=4][b]Games in the catalog[/b][/size]

11 games, 31 versions at the moment:

[list]
[*]The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim Special Edition (10 builds)
[*]The Elder Scrolls V: Skyrim
[*]The Elder Scrolls IV: Oblivion
[*]The Elder Scrolls III: Morrowind
[*]Fallout 4 (2 builds)
[*]Fallout: New Vegas
[*]Fallout 3
[*]Starfield
[*]Cyberpunk 2077 (5 builds)
[*]Baldur's Gate 3 (3 builds)
[*]Stardew Valley (5 builds)
[/list]

The catalog is fetched from the project's repository at startup, so games and builds can be added without shipping a new release. If a lookup fails it falls back to the copy in the download.

[size=4][b]Status[/b][/size]

This is an early release and worth treating as one.

Downloading a chosen version into a fresh folder is the part that has had the most use. Changing an existing install has been verified end to end on single files and on a copy of a real install, both directions hashing exactly to their target manifests, but it has not yet had a lot of mileage across many machines. Point it at something you can afford to reinstall first.

Linux builds are planned. The Windows binary is signed.

[size=4][b]Source[/b][/size]

[url=https://github.com/halgari/reliquary]github.com/halgari/reliquary[/url]. GPL-3.0. Issues and pull requests welcome.

[size=4][b]Not affiliated[/b][/size]

Not associated with or endorsed by Valve Corporation or Steam. Reliquary talks to Steam using your own account and your own licences, and does nothing you could not do with the official client if it exposed the option.
